WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0042: the camel and the water,
a junior-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:10.067
Two parts and a bonus: the question's numbers,
the general formula, and special cases.

3
00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:15.100
At the start there are 450 litres of water,
three full loads of 150.

4
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:20.133
The camel carries up to 150 litres and drinks
half a litre per km, loaded or not.

5
00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:24.133
It may leave water in depots on the way
and come back for more. How far can it get?

6
00:00:24.933 --> 00:00:29.833
Part A: what is the farthest distance
the camel can bring 30 litres to?

7
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:36.033
The first try: three loads, three walks,
so every km costs 1.5 litres.

8
00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:41.333
But between walks the camel goes back
for the next load, and that burns water too.

9
00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:45.733
Five crossings, not three:
at the start every km costs 2.5 litres.

10
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:51.233
A calculation that counts only the walks forward:
a first segment of 100 km, and an answer of 440 km.

11
00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:55.767
But that cannot be reached: the camel
cannot skip the way back.

12
00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:59.767
This is the most common mistake in this puzzle:
counting only the way forward.

13
00:01:00.567 --> 00:01:06.067
The price list: with j loads, j walks forward
and j − 1 back, so 2j − 1 crossings.

14
00:01:06.067 --> 00:01:10.800
Three loads: 2.5 litres per km.
Two: 1.5. One load: 0.5.

15
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.900
A litre at 2.5 per km moves the water
only 1/2.5 km. So litres turn into km.

16
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:21.633
The top load is worth 60 km, the next 100,
and the last 300: 460 in all.

17
00:01:21.633 --> 00:01:27.033
But 30 litres must arrive. In the last load
they are worth 60 km we may not burn.

18
00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:31.433
That leaves 460 − 60 = 400 km.
That is the answer; now the plan.

19
00:01:32.233 --> 00:01:37.733
Where to stop? Say at 50 km, a round number.
After 50 km at 2.5, 325 litres are left.

20
00:01:37.733 --> 00:01:43.233
More than two loads: the next segment still
pays 2.5 for 10 more km, or 25 are spilled.

21
00:01:43.233 --> 00:01:47.233
So stop only when a whole load runs out exactly:
there, and not before, the price drops.

22
00:01:48.033 --> 00:01:53.433
Stop exactly when a whole load runs out:
300 left at 60 km, 150 left at 160.

23
00:01:53.433 --> 00:01:59.033
Segment 1, from 0 to 60: each walk burns 30.
Take 150, arrive with 120, and leave 90.

24
00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:03.633
Go back with 30, exactly what the way back burns,
and reach the start empty.

25
00:02:03.633 --> 00:02:08.367
Take another 150, again arrive with 120
and leave 90. Depot 60 holds 180.

26
00:02:08.367 --> 00:02:12.767
Back again with 30. At the start one last
load is left, 150 litres.

27
00:02:12.767 --> 00:02:18.500
The third time it does not go back and leaves everything:
depot 60 holds 300 litres, two loads.

28
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:22.500
Five crossings of 60 km burned 150 litres,
exactly one load. From here the price is 1.5.

29
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:28.100
Segment 2, from 60 to 160, the same way: each walk
burns 50; out, back and out.

30
00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:32.100
Three crossings of 100 km burned 150:
depot 160 holds 150 litres, one load.

31
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:37.133
Segment 3: one load, no returns.
Walk 240 km, burn 120, and arrive with 30.

32
00:02:37.133 --> 00:02:42.100
400 km, with 30 litres at the target. Check:
every litre was burned on the way or arrived.

33
00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:47.700
The camel crossed the first segment 5 times,
the second 3, and the last once.

34
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.167
840 km walked in all,
to advance 400.

35
00:02:51.967 --> 00:02:57.133
Why no further? The water still in motion
drops from 450 to 30 along the road.

36
00:02:57.133 --> 00:03:01.800
The slope in each segment is the price list:
there is no cheaper way to move water.

37
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.800
Every km burns at least one km of the 460 range,
so 400 is the maximum. The proof is in part B.

38
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:10.633
This is how the page's box shows it:
the passes one after another.

39
00:03:10.633 --> 00:03:14.533
After five passes all the water
is at 60 km: 300 litres.

40
00:03:14.533 --> 00:03:19.667
And the last pass: 30 litres reach
400 km, with the price list under each segment.

41
00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:25.366
Part B: the camel drinks a per km, carries c,
there are w litres, and T must arrive.

42
00:03:26.066 --> 00:03:31.566
The general price list: with N litres in motion
you need ⌈N / c⌉ loads, and each km costs g(N).

43
00:03:31.566 --> 00:03:36.466
The price is a staircase, dropping at every multiple of c.
That is exactly where to stop.

44
00:03:37.066 --> 00:03:42.033
A litre at price g buys 1/g km. The range
of the water is the area under 1/g.

45
00:03:42.033 --> 00:03:46.766
With part A's numbers: 300 + 100 + 60 = 460,
the loads from the bottom up.

46
00:03:46.766 --> 00:03:50.766
The top load is worth the least, 60 km,
because it is carried the most times.

47
00:03:50.766 --> 00:03:55.299
The 30 litres that must arrive are 60
of the area: those are not burned.

48
00:03:55.299 --> 00:04:00.799
The maximum distance is the area between T and w:
the water's range minus what must arrive.

49
00:04:01.599 --> 00:04:06.999
The levels: w, the multiples of c between T and w,
and T. Each two adjacent levels are a segment.

50
00:04:06.999 --> 00:04:11.166
A segment's length: the drop in water
divided by the price of its loads.

51
00:04:11.166 --> 00:04:15.566
In part A: 60, 100 and 240 km,
the plan's three segments.

52
00:04:16.366 --> 00:04:21.666
Each segment has j walks forward. On every walk
but the last the camel takes a full load.

53
00:04:21.666 --> 00:04:25.666
It burns aL on the way, keeps aL for the return,
and leaves c − 2aL at the far depot.

54
00:04:25.666 --> 00:04:30.266
On the last walk it takes what is left,
the partial load, and leaves it all.

55
00:04:30.266 --> 00:04:34.266
2j − 1 crossings of L burn exactly
the drop: the far depot reaches the next level.

56
00:04:35.066 --> 00:04:40.533
Let k be the loads, the top one partial,
of size r: what is left above k − 1 full ones.

57
00:04:40.533 --> 00:04:46.033
Each full load from the bottom is worth c / (2j − 1)a,
the partial one on top r / (2k − 1)a.

58
00:04:46.033 --> 00:04:49.999
And subtract T / a: the km
the water that must arrive is worth.

59
00:04:49.999 --> 00:04:53.999
With T = 0 this is exactly Φ(w): the camel reaches
at most 460 km, empty.

60
00:04:53.999 --> 00:04:58.466
Check against part A: k = 3 and r = 150,
and the formula gives 400.

61
00:04:59.266 --> 00:05:04.766
It remains to prove that no plan, even a clever
one we did not think of, goes further.

62
00:05:04.766 --> 00:05:08.766
The idea: count how much range each km
of road burns, in any plan at all.

63
00:05:09.566 --> 00:05:15.066
Each crossing forward carries at most c, so N litres
need at least ⌈N / c⌉ crossings, with returns between.

64
00:05:15.066 --> 00:05:19.966
So every km costs at least g(N):
the price list is a lower bound on the price.

65
00:05:19.966 --> 00:05:24.566
So in any plan the water in motion drops
at least at rate g, every km.

66
00:05:24.566 --> 00:05:29.299
And by the chain rule, every km of road
burns at least one km of range.

67
00:05:29.299 --> 00:05:33.299
Summed along the road: after d km
at most Φ(w) − d of range is left.

68
00:05:33.299 --> 00:05:38.766
For T litres to arrive, d cannot exceed
Φ(w) − Φ(T). That is exactly the formula.

69
00:05:39.566 --> 00:05:45.066
And the plan that stops at every multiple of c
pays exactly the price list: it reaches the bound.

70
00:05:45.066 --> 00:05:49.066
A stop anywhere else: the camel keeps paying
for a load it no longer needs, or spills water.

71
00:05:49.866 --> 00:05:55.266
The first stop is where the partial load runs out,
and each stop after it where a whole load runs out.

72
00:05:55.266 --> 00:05:59.566
In part A: stops at 60 and 160 km,
exactly as in the plan.

73
00:05:59.566 --> 00:06:05.066
At stop i, (k − i)c litres are left. The last
segment, with one load, is (c − T) / a long.

74
00:06:05.866 --> 00:06:11.266
In the general box you choose consumption, capacity,
water and target. These are part A's numbers.

75
00:06:11.266 --> 00:06:16.399
With 250 litres: a partial load of 100 on top,
a stop at 66.7 km, and a distance of 306.7.

76
00:06:17.199 --> 00:06:21.499
Bonus: three short questions
that check the formula is really understood.

77
00:06:22.199 --> 00:06:27.699
One load: no stops, the camel just walks
until T litres are left. 150 litres: 240.

78
00:06:27.699 --> 00:06:32.999
A target larger than a load: the same principle.
200 litres to the target reach only 126.7 km.

79
00:06:32.999 --> 00:06:38.233
And lots of water: the series 1 + 1/3 + 1/5 ...
diverges, so any distance can be reached.

80
00:06:38.233 --> 00:06:42.233
But slowly: each time the water is multiplied by e
it adds only c / 2a, here 150 km.

81
00:06:43.033 --> 00:06:48.533
In the box: the exact distance against the water,
the approximation dashed. 450 litres: 400 km.

82
00:06:48.533 --> 00:06:52.133
With 1,500 litres, more than three times as much,
only 580 km.

83
00:06:52.133 --> 00:06:55.866
And with 3,000 litres: 683.9 km.
The growth is logarithmic.

84
00:06:56.666 --> 00:07:01.699
To sum up: stop when a whole load runs out,
and reach 400 km with 30 litres.

85
00:07:01.699 --> 00:07:06.099
Try your own water and capacity in the box
on question 0042's page on the site.
